ey "$-4x+3=-4x+3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31ef6d07-7ebc-5067-b0a5-3f361bec8d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-288427" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =x-2 \\\\ 2x-5-x & =x-2-x \\\\ x-5 & =-2 \\\\ x-5+5 & =-2+5 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=x-2$ has the unique solution $x=3.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+9-6x \\\\ 1 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+3 & =-4x+3 \\\\ -4x+3+4x & =-4x+3+4x \\\\ 3 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-4x+3=-4x+3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-4x+3=-4x+3$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f505619a-0f8f-5b06-bcd1-9ed29f3a46e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438094} {:answer/id #uuid "800b7f50-dd11-50d4-910a-43bdf1c22e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f505619a-0f8f-5b06-bcd1-9ed29f3a46e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438094} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d40aab5-2a51-5f28-9398-9e1f95aef724" :answer-field/key "$3x+2=4x+1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439076} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35bdc9da-af4c-51a3-977a-aab317bf1688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438096} {:answer/id #uuid "b473c870-043e-51e2-b1e7-8129f88a2f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35bdc9da-af4c-51a3-977a-aab317bf1688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438096} :answer-field/id #uuid "f63c5f3b-e9f0-5d44-b9b5-69d5b9503e16" :answer-field/key "$2x-5=3x-3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439077} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca3aa2f5-1e64-5717-a73e-d38a34cd3c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438098} {:answer/id #uuid "14f6741b-26e8-5eea-b15d-fa64a12ad311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14f6741b-26e8-5eea-b15d-fa64a12ad311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438099} :answer-field/id #uuid "6515f165-1d78-588e-8621-8c95e0aa4812" :answer-field/key "$5x-4=5x-4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43eccfac-bd80-5f7b-abff-3250eee5dc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-288591" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =4x+1 \\\\ 3x+2-3x & =4x+1-3x \\\\ 2 & =x+1 \\\\ 2-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x+2=4x+1$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =3x-3 \\\\ 2x-5-2x & =3x-3-2x \\\\ -5 & =x-3 \\\\ -5+3 & =x-3+3 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=3x-3$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-4 & =5x-4 \\\\ 5x-4-5x & =5x-4-5x \\\\ -4 & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $5x-4=5x-4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $5x-4=5x-4$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46f8b571-b7ad-5efc-bfb0-6e49db6e3e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450366} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8be265-db8c-50ff-9d68-fe21746b4787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d8be265-db8c-50ff-9d68-fe21746b4787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450367} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad4dbfc-bcc0-5e6d-b6d4-e46c856c03d8" :answer-field/key "Its range is $y>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451137} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f3a4f6-e5c9-5b68-a65e-10a9475ced99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450368} {:answer/id #uuid "a76ed30d-31c8-5203-8dd8-5ec99bf68b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28f3a4f6-e5c9-5b68-a65e-10a9475ced99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450368} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb6c276-d8fd-5138-9a90-6c09789f740d" :answer-field/key "It is an increasing function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451138} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7ba5258-5378-53a5-88db-6afd66703ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450370} {:answer/id #uuid "fc711f4a-7d29-5e54-b376-a1498498fbc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc711f4a-7d29-5e54-b376-a1498498fbc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450371} :answer-field/id #uuid "45d53e54-8c42-56fe-87b8-111b8be08363" :answer-field/key "$f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c7808ae-1f8f-5217-b026-8ee08973156b" :question/math-academy-id "q-28981" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=(0.08)^{x}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(0.08)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4e3b5119c92d02bbceb786fefd238182c7c84ca0f4abc85a19f2df86a6ba267.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement “Its range is $y>0$” is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(0.08)}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement “It is an increasing function” is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements “Its range is $y>0$” and “$f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $” are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241c7bd-4a2b-556f-be9f-8a884addebb9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be00b005-d8ae-5556-989d-9072cc71e838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438100} {:answer/id #uuid "3bbcac9c-60a0-50a4-942c-941011f376c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be00b005-d8ae-5556-989d-9072cc71e838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438100} :answer-field/id #uuid "32c87592-feba-5843-b53f-3dd7cba9448b" :answer-field/key "The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439079} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6abcd6a9-7b5e-5d6b-9996-2c86b49036f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438102} {:answer/id #uuid "8b000f1f-8a8f-5516-94cf-8fdecbb9fac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b000f1f-8a8f-5516-94cf-8fdecbb9fac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438103} :answer-field/id #uuid "988a8e84-3487-5c91-b92c-117dfb072fd4" :answer-field/key "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eae80eb8-ecc1-50d6-bf8d-03a87f7208d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438104} {:answer/id #uuid "0f1d5a9d-8037-593f-a297-14c9b5d70607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eae80eb8-ecc1-50d6-bf8d-03a87f7208d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438104} :answer-field/id #uuid "22795c00-f02a-5d3a-8c66-e44547341575" :answer-field/key "The range of the function is $[-1,1].$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5a3a079-0ec8-5b33-84d2-568517288149" :question/math-academy-id "q-29608" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$” is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).$ is true. The function has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+2n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi +n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )=tan⁡(x).$\n\n- Statement “The range of the function is $[-1,1].$” is false. The range of $y=tan⁡x$ is $(-\\infty ,+\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147498 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df3a798c-f226-5b4f-8d65-4a305de2b087" :knowledge-point/title "Checking ODEs for Exactness"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daff356a-05c7-5413-b4a9-22dd8c231ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465939} {:answer/id #uuid "494c2be5-029b-57ad-a2e0-c99b1d96fdc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daff356a-05c7-5413-b4a9-22dd8c231ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465939} :answer-field/id #uuid "3202f018-29ed-500d-959e-76c6cab125b7" :answer-field/key "$({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+2)\\,\\text{d}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466813} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81a1850a-0ff0-5a27-a2df-03b614277b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465941} {:answer/id #uuid "73fb1274-128f-5ad3-9378-062ea505cca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81a1850a-0ff0-5a27-a2df-03b614277b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465941} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a399a13-687a-5578-9da2-e8e60a8d3d1f" :answer-field/key "$(3xy)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466814} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1764fb8b-5951-50f4-a435-a85c42961dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465943} {:answer/id #uuid "09e071b8-8e60-56c8-8e8b-b575e492b575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09e071b8-8e60-56c8-8e8b-b575e492b575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465944} :answer-field/id #uuid "795091ec-54dd-5546-a4f6-371522d69932" :answer-field/key "$(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70dbdf12-3bc6-5349-9efa-62768bd9b3d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-318629" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+2)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}+y,\\,Q(x,y)=xy+2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+2) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n \nThus, equation $({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+2)\\,\\text{d}y=0$ is not exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n(3xy)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3xy,\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy) \\\\  & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation $(3xy)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y=0$ is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(y-4x)=4x-y,\\,Q(x,y)=6y-x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6y-x) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, equation $(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$ is exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(6y-x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147498 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df3a798c-f226-5b4f-8d65-4a305de2b087" :knowledge-point/title "Checking ODEs for Exactness"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "991c977e-b45f-5410-8eda-0f8842dc0de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465945} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea73e7b-280f-5c50-bec6-7d04449a14ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ea73e7b-280f-5c50-bec6-7d04449a14ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465946} :answer-field/id #uuid "66130991-4adc-539d-8177-a4a5bd39e0ec" :answer-field/key "$(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466816} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61f50423-0ec2-57d0-ab9f-849858f31c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465947} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f97384-e308-5af2-9c38-2575f722e764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61f50423-0ec2-57d0-ab9f-849858f31c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465947} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b8b3d88-bb0e-5d89-adac-eb84c79e516b" :answer-field/key "$({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466817} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8962ecd-3ce2-541f-9931-765f1942f131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465949} {:answer/id #uuid "28d3123a-f2ff-579d-bd7c-5378c5ea42b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8962ecd-3ce2-541f-9931-765f1942f131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465949} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a5f83ce-fbbe-585e-98c9-f0216caf3a08" :answer-field/key "$(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "861223b8-6640-50cd-ba1a-46920dc4bc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-318630" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2xy+5,\\,Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+7.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+5) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+7) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n \nThus, equation $(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0$ is exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{2}+x,\\,Q(x,y)=xy+1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+1) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation $({y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+1)\\,\\text{d}y=0$ is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-(y-2x)=2x-y,\\,Q(x,y)=5y-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5y-3x) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nThus, equation $(5y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y-(y-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0$ is not exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(2xy+5)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+7)\\,\\text{d}y=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79894abb-faad-5931-9d40-faa1f39e5764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450372} {:answer/id #uuid "d5731424-10c5-508a-95c0-b92249c60ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5731424-10c5-508a-95c0-b92249c60ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450373} :answer-field/id #uuid "764dfcac-4876-51f5-bc53-1458e42b1173" :answer-field/key "$A$ is invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451140} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ff86019-aa8e-51d7-91a5-9b1895531b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450374} {:answer/id #uuid "68a95d9a-1a10-55be-a2ff-3c295d5d8313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ff86019-aa8e-51d7-91a5-9b1895531b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450374} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d1f70e-aade-5ae7-863c-9627a3f0f336" :answer-field/key "$det(A)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451141} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18186095-a1e2-5aa9-a6ab-95e5f7d6d82e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450376} {:answer/id #uuid "e1596e7d-7e74-5220-bed2-c83a2733d4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18186095-a1e2-5aa9-a6ab-95e5f7d6d82e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450376} :answer-field/id #uuid "e26d083b-e309-51bf-bb57-63323ba92a01" :answer-field/key "$Ax=0$ has an infinite number of solutions" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a35d343-f492-5478-a8b0-3c966904f7aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-31907" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement $det(A)=0$ is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement “$Ax=0$ has an infinite number of solutions” is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement “$A$ is invertible” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92a6fafe-efb0-5881-ae57-4f6578603ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465951} {:answer/id #uuid "a19942b2-5afd-5b36-9805-4daaad62d850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a19942b2-5afd-5b36-9805-4daaad62d850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465952} :answer-field/id #uuid "048c186b-3e22-53d2-b5cf-73cb9b7e5280" :answer-field/key "$W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466819} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f41dfdf-f241-5b05-b863-2e4f96517509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465953} {:answer/id #uuid "c2318367-e252-5496-9411-127d5517b78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f41dfdf-f241-5b05-b863-2e4f96517509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465953} :answer-field/id #uuid "c06f62ee-1262-52be-bf19-60dea19442c8" :answer-field/key "$Y=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466820} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7d46340-1822-5fed-8361-d6d5e3547c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465955} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2bfce5-db39-5ada-92f7-ade37d95ec31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a2bfce5-db39-5ada-92f7-ade37d95ec31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465956} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0b4c2f3-46b2-5669-ae14-3227a5a0e6f9" :answer-field/key "$Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cbe50951-f70c-5a66-becb-e9afd71bebc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-31968" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $W$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $Y$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $Z$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $W$ and $Z$ are elementary matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffdc31d0-a99c-55cb-a894-d2f460d613a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465957} {:answer/id #uuid "15e3698d-c485-5744-978c-8966c6f1e9c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15e3698d-c485-5744-978c-8966c6f1e9c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465958} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d8e552f-3cde-5d66-b25c-bb83e47de58c" :answer-field/key "$x(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466822} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "973ba0b6-a0c4-55b7-9378-1da1968449b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465959} {:answer/id #uuid "c5bb3961-65af-5f39-842a-0a821fc0d8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "973ba0b6-a0c4-55b7-9378-1da1968449b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465959} :answer-field/id #uuid "43926316-b41f-522a-abf2-5e27e1835beb" :answer-field/key "$x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-t\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466823} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a63401e2-08e9-5087-9feb-36b64e7201f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465961} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf699ad-899d-510a-8ff8-487bf4b05c01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a63401e2-08e9-5087-9feb-36b64e7201f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465961} :answer-field/id #uuid "4062743c-9793-5905-b74e-0d5a3e17ca24" :answer-field/key "$x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "775e3d7f-2959-5adc-b3eb-ee2a98bbde96" :question/math-academy-id "q-321646" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option $x(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+(-1)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-t\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option “$x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)” might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "635db59e-1cbe-5374-93a1-dc5600b113ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465963} {:answer/id #uuid "1380955a-7891-5ebb-8e6f-0ecb7cd8d163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "635db59e-1cbe-5374-93a1-dc5600b113ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465963} :answer-field/id #uuid "747147b5-6f28-516b-9fbd-ed2b6847683b" :answer-field/key "$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c00b5b07-5075-5047-bce9-30f2f0b43b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465965} {:answer/id #uuid "1f347499-a2ef-5193-98a9-edc223ccddfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c00b5b07-5075-5047-bce9-30f2f0b43b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465965} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfb25ace-1008-524c-8fa4-94dc70d5efd6" :answer-field/key "$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466826} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e08924d1-95b8-535a-aad8-72b551226515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465967} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4d68b6-0db1-5d6f-8a30-e9cbd4a836ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd4d68b6-0db1-5d6f-8a30-e9cbd4a836ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465968} :answer-field/id #uuid "09f84aee-b132-58fa-9a7b-d7984b11fcb4" :answer-field/key "$C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "587dcbe8-780f-5ae0-a952-bc8f4458a90a" :question/math-academy-id "q-32176" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e074fd44-241e-5354-ac55-574c46320793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465969} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f83324-3e40-5b90-bd0e-d0bb28e72fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9f83324-3e40-5b90-bd0e-d0bb28e72fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465970} :answer-field/id #uuid "43fc67a8-d442-5472-ade2-018a534151c1" :answer-field/key "$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466828} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66c40a5d-dc92-519c-8c9b-36cc0d76a52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465971} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9eb1fa-cf44-5e53-8e6c-d71c41e60033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c40a5d-dc92-519c-8c9b-36cc0d76a52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465971} :answer-field/id #uuid "33e39b3f-5c6b-5047-bec7-489e6b7713bf" :answer-field/key "$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466829} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d989e3c-b2a7-53a2-84f7-ed72a3c7274b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465973} {:answer/id #uuid "1919dfcb-4715-5a8a-b3ac-9c30fe778b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1919dfcb-4715-5a8a-b3ac-9c30fe778b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465974} :answer-field/id #uuid "09f314cc-c8c7-5588-97e5-e9fdc6aa309a" :answer-field/key "$C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1208e79a-a661-5205-bd4d-ce2da940348a" :question/math-academy-id "q-32177" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block lower triangular?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A block lower triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks above the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block lower triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166970 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9740440-bdfb-588e-8f23-2eec9427e449" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Runge-Kutta Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58722931-7a9c-51fb-b515-dee313825033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465975} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e9de92-470b-54aa-b104-88c38fbe918b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3e9de92-470b-54aa-b104-88c38fbe918b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465976} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e4dfa34-fa5f-5e70-9a91-47a39c73d7dc" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466831} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beb1d7b5-f785-5bc3-b4ea-969a943a627e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465977} {:answer/id #uuid "0efc561a-3ddb-5641-a4bd-3b5a0dbcb23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0efc561a-3ddb-5641-a4bd-3b5a0dbcb23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465978} :answer-field/id #uuid "14b77119-d12c-5899-abaf-43ce62409b63" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466832} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3eaf501-e9d6-592f-bbe5-e3364f8b6f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465979} {:answer/id #uuid "3d7a7e0d-3265-5b01-9b2a-bc64949c7e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3eaf501-e9d6-592f-bbe5-e3364f8b6f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465979} :answer-field/id #uuid "598cb171-6a52-5baf-9743-c8479c663822" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{8})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c780dd-c7ca-58af-8e2e-0a4e9d41e44d" :question/math-academy-id "q-327582" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y).$ The table below shows the intermediate quantities computed in the first iteration of the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nGiven that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ at which of the following points is the function $f$ evaluated during this iteration?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow, we have the following table of values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |\n\nWith this in mind, let's find each point at which the slope is evaluated in turn using the values given in the table.\n\n- The slope ${y}^{′}$ is evaluated at the initial point $(x,y)=({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(0,1).$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{1}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{2}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{3}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{4},\\,1+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the slope is evaluated at the points $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{2})$; $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4})$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04215ae4-e362-5179-aa16-f64611cab85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465981} {:answer/id #uuid "8a73ae01-e668-533a-8ee7-c6b89930becc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a73ae01-e668-533a-8ee7-c6b89930becc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465982} :answer-field/id #uuid "128db81a-f678-5e76-9297-f5984c2d1de0" :answer-field/key "$2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466834} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7817a577-c492-5b10-ad8a-ada69e8ae0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465983} {:answer/id #uuid "5babd41a-b59c-5662-9f2a-5f8f64060536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7817a577-c492-5b10-ad8a-ada69e8ae0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465983} :answer-field/id #uuid "89355e5f-a26a-594a-b62a-a9482525ec6a" :answer-field/key "$-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466835} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d5bc8dc-1011-53d6-be83-2431da52de28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465985} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7d5874-299d-503e-a6ec-50df4a134464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d5bc8dc-1011-53d6-be83-2431da52de28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465985} :answer-field/id #uuid "e260212c-56c8-5314-9942-156986da6d6c" :answer-field/key "$3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f297359-1573-550c-acfb-39ada6af0bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-330297" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64305698-5cb7-58a3-8920-f8133086baec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465987} {:answer/id #uuid "93983b0e-a4d4-570e-8786-0a61bc2fd5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64305698-5cb7-58a3-8920-f8133086baec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465987} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13ae99c-18aa-528c-8e92-0fd5ffbda546" :answer-field/key "$-2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466837} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec6982d1-8cca-5341-aea5-71833a39636c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465989} {:answer/id #uuid "1316fcb8-1c1c-5c1e-aff7-431370a66725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec6982d1-8cca-5341-aea5-71833a39636c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465989} :answer-field/id #uuid "500f98c1-f39c-5a8d-aed2-f064abe90437" :answer-field/key "${x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466838} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "304a041e-9f96-5db9-822e-e1fdc87c43c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465991} {:answer/id #uuid "490bed6d-5c68-5a93-8919-dff31036af0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "490bed6d-5c68-5a93-8919-dff31036af0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465992} :answer-field/id #uuid "38a38067-a95d-55de-aa07-a74dade22f01" :answer-field/key "$-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78db71f4-3fc9-53f6-9a4c-5f66663f6749" :question/math-academy-id "q-330298" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option $-2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $4$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option ${x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option $-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f74d291-c3ec-5bc4-87f6-5aa006c9ebc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465993} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5499f4-34ea-5f2a-8be2-ae18558867c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f74d291-c3ec-5bc4-87f6-5aa006c9ebc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465993} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e1421a3-ab24-57bf-8727-a502d021307f" :answer-field/key "${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466840} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a65cdf03-fec5-5994-9963-81c7e656095a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465995} {:answer/id #uuid "bb95971f-6b94-559b-95a9-14b003051ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a65cdf03-fec5-5994-9963-81c7e656095a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465995} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cc1b2df-65bd-5c58-86b3-00c41699004f" :answer-field/key "$5\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466841} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b9d2e8-1e9b-52a2-bb4e-dc4e64fb5349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465997} {:answer/id #uuid "d5caddf1-dcd5-5bf4-a544-db4b20d03736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05b9d2e8-1e9b-52a2-bb4e-dc4e64fb5349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465997} :answer-field/id #uuid "45c65cce-ff8e-5b99-a907-83dea3f371bc" :answer-field/key "$-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d50adfd6-221a-580f-9d34-b5717546927f" :question/math-academy-id "q-330300" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option $5\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $5\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $5$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed00e399-1ffb-5322-a233-0ae88949f98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465999} {:answer/id #uuid "2557342b-e85e-5a80-b3fa-69cbbedd5ecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2557342b-e85e-5a80-b3fa-69cbbedd5ecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466000} :answer-field/id #uuid "633728be-6b0e-5675-bb30-87e26f4d918e" :answer-field/key "${x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466843} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ff92e7-67c7-5547-bed0-850897cd7252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466001} {:answer/id #uuid "a5992b59-7848-5b0f-a3b4-3c48f56cb8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5992b59-7848-5b0f-a3b4-3c48f56cb8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466002} :answer-field/id #uuid "9207871d-4ce6-5020-9be8-d953e8b91116" :answer-field/key "$-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466844} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66f0526a-67e7-5227-931a-6aacd9525023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466003} {:answer/id #uuid "7791d5f0-de01-531c-8421-69d34d448c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7791d5f0-de01-531c-8421-69d34d448c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466004} :answer-field/id #uuid "e59b0e28-290f-520f-94d6-4f353375a321" :answer-field/key "$7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6d8d0be-fe4e-5e1a-948d-b8eb21d680a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-330302" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option ${x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)={x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}_{1}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option $7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$; $-3\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$; $7\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6b5959-7e82-580f-8ce3-986e9d787885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466005} {:answer/id #uuid "10b79adc-acdf-52b8-bb24-cf229ef3e9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10b79adc-acdf-52b8-bb24-cf229ef3e9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466006} :answer-field/id #uuid "71ca3d40-0366-5608-a056-4c63873a3687" :answer-field/key "$4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466846} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee7e526-5079-566b-b43f-55449205d8f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466007} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3b92e9-33ac-5f2f-bd3b-e25b6e845f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d3b92e9-33ac-5f2f-bd3b-e25b6e845f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466008} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e6580e8-268c-5643-9eeb-68f96909e1a0" :answer-field/key "$-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466847} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b8627b9-3e1e-52d5-99b3-46674a6b9203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466009} {:answer/id #uuid "9f16a7a2-4bda-563c-b9d2-49cc6156f0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b8627b9-3e1e-52d5-99b3-46674a6b9203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466009} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8bdf9e9-39fa-5c00-9b52-2a29ea64ca07" :answer-field/key "${x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63b3f264-c6f0-527c-811d-3ed89e69129d" :question/math-academy-id "q-330305" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option $4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option $-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option ${x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$; $-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c88b0e4e-5a3e-5079-8b0d-dfcc622ee62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466011} {:answer/id #uuid "08086d2f-8b6f-5681-8f08-bde20907f6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08086d2f-8b6f-5681-8f08-bde20907f6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466012} :answer-field/id #uuid "797d260f-5ec6-56eb-bc36-d7cda55f4895" :answer-field/key "$2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466849} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89c338ab-2af4-57e2-ab5f-5093a76750d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466013} {:answer/id #uuid "76aa5d3b-359b-560f-bc95-487efa372d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89c338ab-2af4-57e2-ab5f-5093a76750d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466013} :answer-field/id #uuid "71ccbeb8-7023-5d38-8c4a-7e73be5751bf" :answer-field/key "${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466850} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b37164e3-6c13-55ea-af30-b7e5107db181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466015} {:answer/id #uuid "8be1a8f6-5899-561d-9625-51d49bd0dabc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b37164e3-6c13-55ea-af30-b7e5107db181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466015} :answer-field/id #uuid "589c079d-5042-5aa9-a7fb-bf8c5b6dbfec" :answer-field/key "$3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed465271-2eba-5278-8938-505251fa9eb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-330308" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c8a81a9-aee0-56a4-b68f-2a2e79bd4516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466017} {:answer/id #uuid "345d3f7c-1e64-52cb-b627-5780060ae9c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "345d3f7c-1e64-52cb-b627-5780060ae9c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466018} :answer-field/id #uuid "a12e1ec7-d047-568a-af4b-b20de2971566" :answer-field/key "$-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466852} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d802e20-8c04-5766-bd86-0f77748b6c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466019} {:answer/id #uuid "987c28c3-8e1a-579e-8d11-c523886be91c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "987c28c3-8e1a-579e-8d11-c523886be91c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466020} :answer-field/id #uuid "805d2c07-a9de-52b8-a22a-e1e4e8b45e36" :answer-field/key "$2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466853} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "987acaa7-0bbf-55e8-a5ab-af5abd891933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466021} {:answer/id #uuid "fb503b45-96ed-575b-b767-59b553927311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb503b45-96ed-575b-b767-59b553927311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466022} :answer-field/id #uuid "5754b775-f9a5-55de-9c6f-c8ca3b1a0f41" :answer-field/key "${x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7119481-1ac5-5669-b8e8-7afd953dbdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-330309" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option ${x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)={x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}_{2}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$; $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$; ${x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cef0993c-4542-50d1-85b3-7e4ab1c88cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466023} {:answer/id #uuid "afe220bd-5522-5811-a67a-dd77deb23c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cef0993c-4542-50d1-85b3-7e4ab1c88cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466023} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa33c9fe-228f-5e1f-ba67-6f191bf5bf77" :answer-field/key "$x(t)={t}^{2}\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466855} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64f2895b-3d99-53fe-a08b-3fb528d780b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466025} {:answer/id #uuid "82b170ad-b35c-5c1f-adcd-2ae534c81b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64f2895b-3d99-53fe-a08b-3fb528d780b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466025} :answer-field/id #uuid "93d23d8a-6928-50f0-9072-d270ddd0d007" :answer-field/key "$x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466856} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "655cc7d8-fd02-534e-8b8b-651b45c19606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466027} {:answer/id #uuid "bfeccafa-890c-5f83-87b9-43c32dc005d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfeccafa-890c-5f83-87b9-43c32dc005d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466028} :answer-field/id #uuid "053181da-3026-5a83-a668-828f59259c0c" :answer-field/key "$x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "891dbe2f-f88c-5e79-ae9d-64f7413a702a" :question/math-academy-id "q-330312" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option $x(t)={t}^{2}\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option $x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$ is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=5,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x(t)=-3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f3e49a1-1bfa-5e04-8c17-c8857487da1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466029} {:answer/id #uuid "d3353e28-5730-524e-b188-cbc30f9ff7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f3e49a1-1bfa-5e04-8c17-c8857487da1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466029} :answer-field/id #uuid "32fa50d0-6071-515e-b834-6a08e8fef727" :answer-field/key "$x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466858} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5f51ac8-4df8-5318-b8ad-738279bb82b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466031} {:answer/id #uuid "cb88a9fa-3d2b-5935-bf50-83bed476c581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb88a9fa-3d2b-5935-bf50-83bed476c581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466032} :answer-field/id #uuid "a14d2d04-e31e-5843-867c-0f561fda2bd9" :answer-field/key "$x(t)=-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466859} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc25604f-6d66-554f-9078-a2c52836caf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466033} {:answer/id #uuid "4986099b-8e3a-58d8-b520-7af7c2ec2491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc25604f-6d66-554f-9078-a2c52836caf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466033} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e32f981-d258-539e-a80c-8c4918888146" :answer-field/key "$x(t)={e}^{2t}\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fac4a66c-708b-59cc-844a-c54884651cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-330332" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option $x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier is not a constant.\n\n- Option $x(t)=-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$ is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=0$ and ${c}_{2}=-4,$ respectively.\n\n- Option $x(t)={e}^{2t}\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a scalar multiple of any of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x(t)=-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3f7ae5e-83a5-5eee-b72b-dd7efc63c0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466035} {:answer/id #uuid "517876fa-dd59-539d-a674-2c1f29507c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "517876fa-dd59-539d-a674-2c1f29507c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466036} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef3ca6ba-4998-5c33-8022-e9c4136555bb" :answer-field/key "$x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466861} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "878ce675-1899-51cd-a7d3-a9921b8ccea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466037} {:answer/id #uuid "1f12b393-c48e-5a94-bc60-684111e47dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "878ce675-1899-51cd-a7d3-a9921b8ccea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466037} :answer-field/id #uuid "43807068-414e-57ce-9e12-5b33638cbb01" :answer-field/key "$x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466862} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aa9028e-ae3b-5d19-8c27-9fe05f3db2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466039} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf982e7-287d-57cb-b33a-12647e5cbc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9aa9028e-ae3b-5d19-8c27-9fe05f3db2a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466039} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bdfa6da-adb2-51cc-a641-4e5b855165cf" :answer-field/key "$x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89894e08-2da2-5128-a91b-b1054d28b3f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-330353" :question/problem "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option “$x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)” might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166970 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9740440-bdfb-588e-8f23-2eec9427e449" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Runge-Kutta Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfa7cb1a-07d7-5dc1-8522-44e203a20733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466041} {:answer/id #uuid "9777dad2-5fb7-5723-acbd-2c0bc4c3936a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9777dad2-5fb7-5723-acbd-2c0bc4c3936a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466042} :answer-field/id #uuid "419a60db-e566-534a-950d-ae6b92923121" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466864} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b53c0643-3a96-5862-b0b3-ff8d4fdc399b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466043} {:answer/id #uuid "852e28cd-c93d-5074-b2ad-ae17a24423ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "852e28cd-c93d-5074-b2ad-ae17a24423ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466044} :answer-field/id #uuid "742f823c-da15-5177-97f2-f825acd977b5" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{3}{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466865} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff1b42cd-6740-5af8-9a96-f5de1d4f6464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466045} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8b6658-8528-5b51-afe2-a95977740b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8b6658-8528-5b51-afe2-a95977740b17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466046} :answer-field/id #uuid "7002e7f3-60ce-528e-8c65-e48e488b206f" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{3}{2},2)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9b7930e-1624-5550-b77a-3086492ab273" :question/math-academy-id "q-331060" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y).$ The table below shows the intermediate quantities computed in the first iteration of the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $3$ |  |\n\nGiven that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ at which of the following points is the function $f$ evaluated during this iteration?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow, we have the following table of values:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $3$ |  |\n\nWith this in mind, let's find each point at which the slope is evaluated in turn using the values given in the table.\n\n- The slope ${y}^{′}$ is evaluated at the initial point $(x,y)=({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(1,2).$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{1}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{2}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The estimated slope ${y}_{3}^{′}$ is evaluated at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2},\\,2+0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2},2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the slope is evaluated at the points $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{5}{2})$; $(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{3}{2})$; $(\\frac{3}{2},2)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc2f3c2c-fd0f-5a5f-880a-82f786d2fea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466047} {:answer/id #uuid "5eafe294-cd87-5ce6-80c3-61c5a84dab38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc2f3c2c-fd0f-5a5f-880a-82f786d2fea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466047} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a05091-6793-5cc0-b4eb-1ce640b010f7" :answer-field/key "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466867} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d27ea8d-6973-5d55-8a0b-9bf18916d7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466049} {:answer/id #uuid "150d1418-d007-5c9d-a8f6-7a8d44e86905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "150d1418-d007-5c9d-a8f6-7a8d44e86905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466050} :answer-field/id #uuid "55f68aea-74bc-534e-824e-8c810de35f10" :answer-field/key "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-8{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466868} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "364859a7-aaff-5174-bdd3-5f50d200ce9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466051} {:answer/id #uuid "ce486bda-79e9-5dcc-b216-bbf4e590960e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "364859a7-aaff-5174-bdd3-5f50d200ce9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466051} :answer-field/id #uuid "4abec42a-e2c5-5ff8-8ec3-56a8bfa4ed73" :answer-field/key "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "363ad515-bb3d-517b-aff5-449014ea0698" :question/math-academy-id "q-331114" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-8{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-8{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-8{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ade60bd6-89a3-5a73-972d-872bbec9ef1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438118} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbc21da-935d-542a-8ab1-73ad0fbda9fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438119}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dbc21da-935d-542a-8ab1-73ad0fbda9fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438119} :answer-field/id #uuid "0358c23b-ddd3-5e03-9d10-c033127c1b43" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3efe629-e162-5be1-b2a1-ef4c42a89b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438120} {:answer/id #uuid "a45bc3bf-30fc-52e0-93fe-132d6f93b6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3efe629-e162-5be1-b2a1-ef4c42a89b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438120} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dfb15d0-be1a-5c7b-a003-222aab29fafc" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5508790c-26b9-5808-affc-0f5c42fd9819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438122} {:answer/id #uuid "40377e57-9072-5c7f-9b8d-750b0bacdd09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5508790c-26b9-5808-affc-0f5c42fd9819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438122} :answer-field/id #uuid "118e5a32-cc2b-560d-ac23-990fe960a18c" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52b69d13-8d12-5e35-b5cc-e11a7f6b65a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331605" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=3x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation has the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff0321f7-e164-5498-b196-4c286eaf7bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438124} {:answer/id #uuid "ba350085-c43f-5ce4-8a62-63ec28159573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff0321f7-e164-5498-b196-4c286eaf7bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438124} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d7d5355-19b7-54c4-8b09-d85e8e57729e" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439091} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e337d58-491f-5879-bf5b-e65fb1c3eb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438126} {:answer/id #uuid "0da375b9-4261-5cc0-b3fe-b7c866eea9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0da375b9-4261-5cc0-b3fe-b7c866eea9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438127} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0e14497-143c-5934-8cfa-95c35be4fb08" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89b67917-bd3d-5f65-9067-a429d1042297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438128} {:answer/id #uuid "98208928-1e1c-5c7d-aa97-3ae58d1c5cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98208928-1e1c-5c7d-aa97-3ae58d1c5cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438129} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af9d526-9783-5010-9162-846dfff432a0" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ed9d4c5-c8ba-5702-acc9-702d6e5cd248" :question/math-academy-id "q-331724" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{2}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{3}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y$; ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22b30a22-e8bf-521c-90a6-3f4a193abff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438130} {:answer/id #uuid "21794aaa-14c3-5e8a-bbaa-2761662e74b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21794aaa-14c3-5e8a-bbaa-2761662e74b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438131} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd5b6aa-8b92-5f0c-ad2c-a55d462aa082" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy=-y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439094} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab3067f1-1415-59b1-8610-cf2333e9d171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438132} {:answer/id #uuid "7a90b93a-6c14-52f2-bdf3-931241711cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a90b93a-6c14-52f2-bdf3-931241711cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438133} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a1dfa1a-de10-5f44-9760-65dd587cb80b" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439095} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d659f16-e228-5cec-abc7-98ba7fc32f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438134} {:answer/id #uuid "5438dd0d-7002-5197-8393-206d7d4887ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5438dd0d-7002-5197-8393-206d7d4887ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438135} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c171570-be09-53d8-bdb3-f8add2aaf918" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-xy=-5y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4c450ce-db9f-5997-8c48-e9942b8b8389" :question/math-academy-id "q-331725" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-5y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-5y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+5y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-5)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}-xy=-y$; ${y}^{′}-xy=0$; ${y}^{′}-xy=-5y$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a05adb9-4f73-5867-bae3-ff6175a13e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438136} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0154cf-98d8-5cc7-9bac-150731181edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a0154cf-98d8-5cc7-9bac-150731181edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438137} :answer-field/id #uuid "a41ee71f-fd54-5303-b163-f2032d6801c3" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439097} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29064d13-7994-5b10-9af4-dbca4742bcfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438138} {:answer/id #uuid "8fef5b4b-662f-579a-bd84-e9fad058b00a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29064d13-7994-5b10-9af4-dbca4742bcfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438138} :answer-field/id #uuid "a94578c7-4ce4-5913-a0af-1a5e8cbee912" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439098} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f454d491-4ca7-5ed2-8988-fdbd860e0db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438140} {:answer/id #uuid "b48480a2-0492-52d3-a196-2a8da93df95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b48480a2-0492-52d3-a196-2a8da93df95b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438141} :answer-field/id #uuid "03434b70-7877-52d6-9480-68bedbdb7560" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb41985-a448-5edf-ac16-5140f9df994f" :question/math-academy-id "q-331727" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y$; ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "614bde2f-3b34-55b7-8979-0bbe0e0e8d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438142} {:answer/id #uuid "2440c20b-4602-5338-a005-b76f98501570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2440c20b-4602-5338-a005-b76f98501570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438143} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1a50845-014d-5d37-b7ad-8ae39954a63e" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-4xy=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439100} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50f411e6-4387-5475-b44a-3ebe176b8d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438144} {:answer/id #uuid "acaef6e2-a03c-5a66-aae6-11b882603afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acaef6e2-a03c-5a66-aae6-11b882603afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438145} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c7d2e1e-2a32-5f22-89f7-d692d818f8c4" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-4xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439101} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1bd087a-665c-563f-a5fa-648107296f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438146} {:answer/id #uuid "f1acffb4-55b8-551e-b237-9deee744667f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1bd087a-665c-563f-a5fa-648107296f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438146} :answer-field/id #uuid "c735e342-a746-5fcf-8d38-1e9b66bb4050" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-4xy=9$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70f3dd1b-d16f-5aa1-9eba-10b4e0fd71e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331728" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(4x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=9.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $9$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}-4xy=y$; ${y}^{′}-4xy=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "659f5dad-d96d-572c-bc0e-6839e5d2f979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438148} {:answer/id #uuid "26b77c0c-415c-551c-9af2-e73c88f28745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "659f5dad-d96d-572c-bc0e-6839e5d2f979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438148} :answer-field/id #uuid "8612dfff-4796-54a5-a331-df6cd79613e9" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+7xy=5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439103} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbc86210-2f25-55e4-979f-8cd8499bd6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438150} {:answer/id #uuid "4eafb62b-02d1-5dba-aea3-5813048a7402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eafb62b-02d1-5dba-aea3-5813048a7402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438151} :answer-field/id #uuid "581545d9-353d-50b3-8961-7af4bb789164" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+7xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439104} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46b6717f-cd6d-5839-8478-f93130f282fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438152} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc30338-2228-5ddc-91d4-fa268dd71886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdc30338-2228-5ddc-91d4-fa268dd71886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438153} :answer-field/id #uuid "b69443e9-7deb-5890-aee3-96bd29b55c46" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+7xy=-2y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8cfe21c-18ac-5f6d-9859-3a5cbe483542" :question/math-academy-id "q-331729" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=5.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $5$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=-2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+7xy & =-2y \\\\ {y}^{′}+7xy+2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(7x+2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+7xy=0$; ${y}^{′}+7xy=-2y$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "667d5f81-9239-59f3-bd64-e30a13824e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438154} {:answer/id #uuid "320f233a-3b54-548f-96cb-6b5c4aa6ac98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "320f233a-3b54-548f-96cb-6b5c4aa6ac98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438155} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c546fc3-85f8-5495-bac0-790330521078" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+4xy=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439106} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cc51b98-5b0c-5884-b8ee-f552971ac473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438156} {:answer/id #uuid "f499baaa-7017-54d1-957c-4660554066fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cc51b98-5b0c-5884-b8ee-f552971ac473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438156} :answer-field/id #uuid "03137208-0438-51b2-9ef5-6db630037564" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+4xy=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439107} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "345d36c9-0fc8-53ad-b297-f2466b7f6b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438158} {:answer/id #uuid "749d5e36-3386-5223-bb66-49011d5f7f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "345d36c9-0fc8-53ad-b297-f2466b7f6b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438158} :answer-field/id #uuid "093b0f41-e3f6-5616-abd6-6d38edcb4b50" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+4xy=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e88e764-3128-53c6-9d2a-d1f58dae12a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331738" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(4x-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+4xy=2$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60013046-6869-55cf-91f7-48420115bedd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438160} {:answer/id #uuid "1722b0bf-64b9-51d2-999e-515e7b90211b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1722b0bf-64b9-51d2-999e-515e7b90211b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438161} :answer-field/id #uuid "a97231ab-1b29-58c9-ac31-9f761a81bcc6" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "826ce20d-1cce-5586-9508-6cbc2e7afe87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438162} {:answer/id #uuid "795a6de7-fdd5-572b-8fcb-d6f2d8543bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "826ce20d-1cce-5586-9508-6cbc2e7afe87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438162} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9a1df3e-533f-5e3d-84b5-f5af94ba6d6e" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dda6491-25bc-5b4a-9996-e5ade3c17097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438164} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb81709-b7ca-5a51-98e8-419baa95af13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dda6491-25bc-5b4a-9996-e5ade3c17097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438164} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3f50ef3-6ae4-5f6f-855a-f7c193330d33" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4b9025a-0c2a-511c-b23e-f617ee7031f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331739" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-({x}^{2}+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8061805c-8cd8-5cc7-bb13-3ad6f9b44f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438166} {:answer/id #uuid "21688abb-cb3b-5a82-a801-8feb6d03c650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8061805c-8cd8-5cc7-bb13-3ad6f9b44f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438166} :answer-field/id #uuid "343bed01-1c21-5af3-bf76-63d75646e463" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2y=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439112} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4441feeb-02b9-5ed8-bcf7-76369fb709ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438168} {:answer/id #uuid "b00c337c-80cb-57cb-9d4a-4a84e893786c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b00c337c-80cb-57cb-9d4a-4a84e893786c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438169} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef31dc0a-a2e6-5f82-be3b-19b7341583d4" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2y=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc31299-2229-5ec9-aa16-a216dd4411f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438170} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb79f69-b06c-55a2-a77c-653ce60b8e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cc31299-2229-5ec9-aa16-a216dd4411f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438170} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce053aea-15b4-561f-8d1f-f208bfa7025d" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+2y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca0875e1-4252-5a69-bfc7-c43bf6770e82" :question/math-academy-id "q-331742" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+2y=x$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de390821-11f1-5f36-9802-7d4b56d82028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438172} {:answer/id #uuid "d04a28d1-2ea2-50b4-af54-7f274cf2e92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d04a28d1-2ea2-50b4-af54-7f274cf2e92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438173} :answer-field/id #uuid "23121eb7-b5f4-5882-9338-16b6fc23ce13" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439115} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9d53b05-9b80-586f-9265-75256fd575c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438174} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a199eb-df36-5457-bf67-8f218b663119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9d53b05-9b80-586f-9265-75256fd575c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438174} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae73da4d-fa51-5351-b7c6-87495ce3cf51" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439116} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57b9dd9c-e174-5c3a-baa9-4fa6ecadc55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438176} {:answer/id #uuid "f01d4288-b6aa-54b7-91cc-7165a1615c2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57b9dd9c-e174-5c3a-baa9-4fa6ecadc55f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438176} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf46d30a-3628-5fcf-8a13-6888631fa4e5" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26251e42-10da-5a5f-8853-15a03bb82549" :question/math-academy-id "q-331743" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=-2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fde37f96-d46e-5cee-9ab6-aab431ae55be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466053} {:answer/id #uuid "2699930e-d019-57fc-8022-2330d11ba5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fde37f96-d46e-5cee-9ab6-aab431ae55be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466053} :answer-field/id #uuid "9199a8be-d8cd-521d-aac3-8b79249a4c7e" :answer-field/key "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466870} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9767513f-6a1c-5067-8d17-1a73876affa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466055} {:answer/id #uuid "db919368-5b43-5056-b22a-11edeb465f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9767513f-6a1c-5067-8d17-1a73876affa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466055} :answer-field/id #uuid "69419699-98d7-52af-997a-ad1c53e03a9b" :answer-field/key "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466871} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22581b05-f6ca-56cf-9273-21259bfec6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466057} {:answer/id #uuid "3227479f-2c9a-502d-bda7-ec628fe6ba9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3227479f-2c9a-502d-bda7-ec628fe6ba9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466058} :answer-field/id #uuid "3be0b716-41e6-5218-9989-d96369208af2" :answer-field/key "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "164f3277-5bbd-5470-9288-18ef536cb19b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331974" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d810721-64de-536e-8ec7-44fde4184215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466059} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6fec79-351c-59dc-8c17-b118e54669f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb6fec79-351c-59dc-8c17-b118e54669f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466060} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b061f2-eb0c-52ec-995e-e755959f6065" :answer-field/key "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466873} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "384b8250-df38-58c3-bf55-21334be56f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466061} {:answer/id #uuid "02da0336-291f-5f57-90d0-d178e7f896ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "384b8250-df38-58c3-bf55-21334be56f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466061} :answer-field/id #uuid "c41e7cca-531f-5697-b5a4-601c968d6d43" :answer-field/key "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466874} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74bf66fe-9855-5a8d-891f-9c88a9651c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466063} {:answer/id #uuid "2282a1cd-d3ab-590a-9a34-f31c344ca632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74bf66fe-9855-5a8d-891f-9c88a9651c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466063} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e3c2c46-97b3-559d-97ab-d711e3149ccf" :answer-field/key "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfb33e06-625f-5894-a78d-d1f418d2b822" :question/math-academy-id "q-331976" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a943d79-9e34-5e46-ad5c-c4f9a85775b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450378} {:answer/id #uuid "71cf041b-4be6-5042-842f-773db685c95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a943d79-9e34-5e46-ad5c-c4f9a85775b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450378} :answer-field/id #uuid "59ff4677-7cec-55b4-8173-8c62481555a5" :answer-field/key "If $f(t)$ contains a discontinuity for some value of $t>0,$ we have $F(s)=0$ for all $s>a.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451143} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6fcd7d1-78b1-5d5e-bdab-c7a25d73350a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450380} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2923a2-591b-5391-b0b1-668944856b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec2923a2-591b-5391-b0b1-668944856b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450381} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c43945e-1718-5308-8edd-3e21aa6ad2c4" :answer-field/key "$F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable on $s>a.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451144} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e5fc112-d65d-5614-a9fc-d206ac21a24a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450382} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff10aa5-d116-583b-a74e-5ebd580c7276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ff10aa5-d116-583b-a74e-5ebd580c7276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450383} :answer-field/id #uuid "53bbf159-0151-5e3d-8f49-4d731d312c32" :answer-field/key "The Laplace transform converts a non-smooth $f(t)$ into a smooth function of $s.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0df5f53a-0779-5ce7-90dc-03bd51afb4c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332674" :question/problem "Let $f(t)$ be a piecewise function defined on $[0,\\infty )$ with exponential order $a,$ and consider its Laplace transform \n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)},\\,s>a.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $f(t)$ contains a discontinuity for some value of $t>0,$ we have $F(s)=0$ for all $s>a.$” is false. Smoothness has no bearing on whether the Laplace transform vanishes.\nA function of exponential order always has a well-defined Laplace transform for $s>a$, and there is no reason for that transform to be identically zero.\n\n- Statement “$F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable on $s>a.$” is true. The smoothness theorem states that for $f(t)$ of exponential order, the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f}(s)\n$$\n\nis infinitely differentiable at every point in its region of convergence, and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}(s).\n$$\n\n- Statement “The Laplace transform converts a non-smooth $f(t)$ into a smooth function of $s.$” is true. The Laplace transform $F(s)$ of a function $f(t)$ is always infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence, even if $f(t)$ is non-smooth.\n\nTherefore, the answer is “$F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable on $s>a.$”; “The Laplace transform converts a non-smooth $f(t)$ into a smooth function of $s.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e50e8ab-e411-586f-a3d4-dc2e4fcd7cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466065} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8afd2e-ae6c-570d-a8ff-76907c67d059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc8afd2e-ae6c-570d-a8ff-76907c67d059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466066} :answer-field/id #uuid "62b9e8dd-90d0-51c7-be44-1b2eb0a32755" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=0.4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466876} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c56cefe7-8454-534e-b476-cc7a1d062420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466067} {:answer/id #uuid "0cfa897d-e2a7-5a0a-9895-d4e337ada051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c56cefe7-8454-534e-b476-cc7a1d062420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466067} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1061fc5-5342-526c-88c1-d457329519a0" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=2.0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e25c152c-cde8-5b9b-a39d-1c434bef3856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466069} {:answer/id #uuid "2db53487-34f5-58b2-a4f4-a19aaa44c64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e25c152c-cde8-5b9b-a39d-1c434bef3856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466069} :answer-field/id #uuid "c682ebed-3244-5b41-a799-d3bf75036c38" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=2.4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3833cd4-b47d-541f-9a24-3a41b77197eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466071} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5fdda5-251a-5d00-8098-412f1be153f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c5fdda5-251a-5d00-8098-412f1be153f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466072} :answer-field/id #uuid "030e73ec-670f-572f-9967-01c8e343d51e" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=1.2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30787f50-63d0-5577-9588-239251d45d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-333092" :question/problem "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.0$ falls *outside* of the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.4$ falls *outside* the given region, and\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ falls *within* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is stable for $\\lambda Δx=0.4$ and $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\lambda Δx=0.4$; $\\lambda Δx=1.2$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c054bd0-f5aa-5673-839d-60c70e36ecde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466073} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec5a82a-15e5-55c1-9ec0-77d8f98d7390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ec5a82a-15e5-55c1-9ec0-77d8f98d7390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466074} :answer-field/id #uuid "304a716b-ed43-5878-9709-b47f3885c2e2" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3y+x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466880} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4196f37b-b17e-5500-b365-38933d8c6fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466075} {:answer/id #uuid "ae04d060-cb4c-53f4-abc0-3ea635425742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4196f37b-b17e-5500-b365-38933d8c6fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466075} :answer-field/id #uuid "b921d865-c281-522b-a275-43c0069d9b9c" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466881} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "309ea663-119a-555b-9de9-53704383119a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466077} {:answer/id #uuid "280dd247-2fa0-514a-be07-100c657f77b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "309ea663-119a-555b-9de9-53704383119a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466077} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a8d88b1-cd72-5a27-b151-965e6f804fb3" :answer-field/key "$y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ad56467-9a9c-5515-8f2a-3d6fb0d92b9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333311" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3y+x$ is a Riccati differential equation with \n\n$$\na(x)={x}^{2}+1,\\,b(x)=-3,\\,c(x)=x.\n$$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1$ is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+5$ is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{5}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{5}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3y+x$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8aa7472-bbd4-5c0a-9f72-9ce28aa1f610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466079} {:answer/id #uuid "24eb1ff0-d74d-5df8-8296-0484e57e9a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8aa7472-bbd4-5c0a-9f72-9ce28aa1f610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466079} :answer-field/id #uuid "85fa4d6e-455a-5cdd-a6be-26c7d529fe1b" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466883} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe67d143-10d1-52d5-8bda-e97f745215c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466081} {:answer/id #uuid "22997aaf-13ec-5c18-9723-c27e1c92bb1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe67d143-10d1-52d5-8bda-e97f745215c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466081} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31af6a4-ddb6-5d83-a0be-d42d0844a09d" :answer-field/key "$y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8130c4e-6214-583b-b0a0-b674a23426a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466083} {:answer/id #uuid "999fc675-5d81-54ab-9955-d557da83a00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "999fc675-5d81-54ab-9955-d557da83a00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466084} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c9f982c-7369-5ecb-a85e-b5422d0616bf" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-4y=(x-2){y}^{2}+{e}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2271166c-28ab-5bb4-b5b8-f402b78156b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333316" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+5$ is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+5.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation $y{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4$ is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{4}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{4}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}-4y=(x-2){y}^{2}+{e}^{x}$ is a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-2){y}^{2}+{e}^{x}+4y.\n$$\n\nThis is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=x-2,\\,b(x)=4,\\,c(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}-4y=(x-2){y}^{2}+{e}^{x}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8b89f26-adad-575c-8f8a-18624e38817d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466085} {:answer/id #uuid "8c088e93-db28-5af4-8cea-02634faa9f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8b89f26-adad-575c-8f8a-18624e38817d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466085} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ee4c8eb-22be-53e4-a3bc-6b780a4d30a7" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-xy+1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466886} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52f914e7-a1ae-5e28-a415-434c06a1f1de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466087} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d23d7e-183c-5ff9-8180-a24422676acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0d23d7e-183c-5ff9-8180-a24422676acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466088} :answer-field/id #uuid "49582718-6055-571a-a46c-d4af70065cb3" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+3){y}^{2}-4y+{e}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466887} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf65d349-d615-5d9f-b082-913281fe1aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466089} {:answer/id #uuid "28309f62-d15b-5b18-8620-3195fd791c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf65d349-d615-5d9f-b082-913281fe1aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466089} :answer-field/id #uuid "18eb13b6-180c-5209-be56-839868507b00" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2cc3f6c-b7da-5ffb-9443-884962200fc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-333321" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-xy+1$ is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-xy+1.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+3){y}^{2}-4y+{e}^{x}$ is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)={x}^{2}+3,\\,b(x)=-4,\\,c(x)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}$ is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+3){y}^{2}-4y+{e}^{x}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57c0a1cb-fb61-52f6-a966-f47b0984daa0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Riccati Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c392cf1-1167-5adf-a2ad-cb7ba649470e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466091} {:answer/id #uuid "c91d6800-6a34-5e04-9e4d-09fdc446964d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c91d6800-6a34-5e04-9e4d-09fdc446964d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466092} :answer-field/id #uuid "3773776b-c4fa-5eaa-894f-b159e277b227" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}+2xy+{x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466889} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84eedbce-c9a4-5756-850f-9f31a1f96a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466093} {:answer/id #uuid "2170eb96-db98-5f50-a545-b496817ebbc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84eedbce-c9a4-5756-850f-9f31a1f96a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466093} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e444b71-ef3e-5e45-ae6a-dd1ac93fd8a9" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}-y+{e}^{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466890} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f411f329-9c25-5838-9474-9b33f4fb1845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466095} {:answer/id #uuid "b827daf3-b4aa-52f6-9229-2fd4e815ba9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f411f329-9c25-5838-9474-9b33f4fb1845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466095} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0b54d48-e87f-5904-8ac7-665b250fd231" :answer-field/key "$y{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f788c2b2-09c8-57a5-9839-705d54ca27d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333322" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}+2xy+{x}^{2}$ is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=x-1,\\,b(x)=2x,\\,c(x)={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}-y+{e}^{x}$ is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}-y+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation $y{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1){y}^{2}-3$ is not a Riccati differential equation.Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1)y-\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{3}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}+2xy+{x}^{2}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d531f41-03d1-5f8a-b99c-4fcb2439b767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450384} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c8f83c-d5b8-51f9-8162-dadf9d36ee63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d531f41-03d1-5f8a-b99c-4fcb2439b767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450384} :answer-field/id #uuid "be5b8e14-c5b0-5c4b-95ab-4b819237e981" :answer-field/key "The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451146} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ebf1794-dd01-555e-96c7-a7ae089c89bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450386} {:answer/id #uuid "363209f5-ad30-5800-8a06-ae8f84e8efde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ebf1794-dd01-555e-96c7-a7ae089c89bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450386} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e7fb117-7117-5d26-990a-f7852a87ead6" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|>1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4803b3d9-920e-5622-935b-02beb52674a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450388} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3c8d02-5e8c-58d5-ad2b-c2793761cc69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f3c8d02-5e8c-58d5-ad2b-c2793761cc69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450389} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ba31bf-db1d-5a3d-b242-c4484fb48ed9" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451148} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae417908-c0fa-5671-acc5-d71f00848463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450390} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3309eb-216a-5b7d-90c0-aaf606aeb4a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb3309eb-216a-5b7d-90c0-aaf606aeb4a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450391} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd6aa78c-584f-5b2b-aa83-d6d6710c377c" :answer-field/key "The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86209978-f558-5e50-81ba-e94e00e9e46b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333340" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=3,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{7}{3},$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=3,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{7}{3}y,\\,y(0)=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{7}{3}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{3}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$” is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)((1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =3{(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement “The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|>1.$” is false.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{7}{3}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{7}{3}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$” is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.9$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$” is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$”; “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c0f79f7-7a42-5f0a-acea-ee182e1d0884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450392} {:answer/id #uuid "486c4858-9e53-5f4a-9101-0a156eaeafd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c0f79f7-7a42-5f0a-acea-ee182e1d0884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450392} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a9b856c-9269-5a16-a3e6-0ebe2c77f23d" :answer-field/key "The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{3}{2}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451150} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7ab8b7-0954-5970-abd1-6f3a716e14d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450394} {:answer/id #uuid "eccac1e7-dfdb-52a9-b414-4362130d31ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eccac1e7-dfdb-52a9-b414-4362130d31ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text}