r/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b9fafa5-80d9-5ee0-9073-4740bef21b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450406} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5e7a1a0-71f4-55ee-a17d-4a5bb7f1501d" :answer-field/key "The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.6.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7bf24239-c81d-5a71-bce2-db470e9ad2d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333347" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{9}{4},$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{9}{4}y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{9}{4}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{9}{4}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n}.$” is true.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)((1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement “The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{9}{4}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{9}{4}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$” is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.6$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.6.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx){y}_{n}.$”; “The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{9}{4}Δx|<1.$”; “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bdd2ceb-c14a-5a3d-bf03-692a1475fa41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0785630f-c4a0-5eaf-8fa5-9797620d11b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450408} {:answer/id #uuid "bcae98ef-55c0-548c-b59c-d0d848ec2dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0785630f-c4a0-5eaf-8fa5-9797620d11b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450408} :answer-field/id #uuid "992e87f4-b99e-55ec-9820-8e8617158600" :answer-field/key "The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451158} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10f97064-9aa9-5c08-beb8-e4b4a48d1d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450410} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae283f3-e1b3-5994-83d2-bc4f5f27c96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ae283f3-e1b3-5994-83d2-bc4f5f27c96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450411} :answer-field/id #uuid "309b3c61-7793-5746-a36a-8bc33cbee8b9" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451159} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0b316ab-f347-5d0d-8694-c4f892fe0a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450412} {:answer/id #uuid "5c79fc57-b06e-544f-9ab7-6e4500203853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c79fc57-b06e-544f-9ab7-6e4500203853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450413} :answer-field/id #uuid "257a5dca-a0c6-55a3-9d39-56ffe4b3b894" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.25.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451160} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc77eac4-c83e-5e6f-8c90-73674eb7fcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450414} {:answer/id #uuid "9ffdd7d9-0412-56f1-9b62-7b22d9d6d115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ffdd7d9-0412-56f1-9b62-7b22d9d6d115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450415} :answer-field/id #uuid "bca4e6b7-f12b-5e4a-b82e-ba01c54504a0" :answer-field/key "The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.8.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4be1bdfb-f8fa-566e-b0ec-d9b21f97e58a" :question/math-academy-id "q-333398" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=6,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{8}{3},$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=6,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{3}y,\\,y(0)=6.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{8}{3}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{8}{3}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$” is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)((1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =6{(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement “The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{8}{3}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{8}{3}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.25$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.25.$” is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.8$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.8.$” is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1.$”; “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.25.$”; “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.8.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a487545d-688f-5289-8f0b-cbe100c0ba5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450416} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a8775d-6db6-5e8d-9a95-4e08eb57d30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a487545d-688f-5289-8f0b-cbe100c0ba5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450416} :answer-field/id #uuid "c23dbd77-b1dd-5cac-ba87-e3f1995bfdfd" :answer-field/key "The stability function is $R(6Δx)=\\frac{1}{1-6Δx}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451162} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8732312-2977-5206-8aa3-d38e25efeeec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450418} {:answer/id #uuid "c5446f44-23e7-56c8-99fa-59812e4e36e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5446f44-23e7-56c8-99fa-59812e4e36e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450419} :answer-field/id #uuid "96632ae8-2955-51e7-b582-cbb1098b7646" :answer-field/key "The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451163} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e466d73-29bb-543b-82ff-2fea8f4f2800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450420} {:answer/id #uuid "431a252e-167f-5417-bfce-d312e3dc47cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "431a252e-167f-5417-bfce-d312e3dc47cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450421} :answer-field/id #uuid "afccbde1-4f2d-555d-b44c-97aabd9ff091" :answer-field/key "The method is absolutely stable." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451164} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a74ff6c2-72a5-5a9d-9c2c-4be8534ba335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450422} {:answer/id #uuid "66e2d1e9-4163-55b9-b8a0-dee0940cd20b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a74ff6c2-72a5-5a9d-9c2c-4be8534ba335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450422} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed64aaa6-6b3f-5c01-83bf-d853788cb46e" :answer-field/key "The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.10.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed443d52-d157-5213-99c2-1ffa8b393121" :question/math-academy-id "q-333546" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =6,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-6y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-6{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+6{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+6Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(6Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}.\n$$\n\nThis shows that the statement “The stability function is $R(6Δx)=\\frac{1}{1-6Δx}.$” is false.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(6Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement “The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+6Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.10.$ Hence, statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.10.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$”; “The method is absolutely stable” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "554a3f25-c6d4-56f1-97a1-e72f6e996868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450424} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf7d503-a537-5980-a459-e3d845c2a7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bf7d503-a537-5980-a459-e3d845c2a7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450425} :answer-field/id #uuid "2668b372-5058-5bcf-87d1-b3dc18aa9e3e" :answer-field/key "The stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1d305cf-a373-552c-84d7-e16b441a5d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450426} {:answer/id #uuid "20b79c3e-cfe8-5182-8d84-c0226c536bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20b79c3e-cfe8-5182-8d84-c0226c536bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450427} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc9d7e34-4fe0-5a4b-be27-0a630b6ca736" :answer-field/key "The stability region is $|\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a745d4d-5653-50dd-8c45-7098cb48f439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450428} {:answer/id #uuid "05afd7a7-372e-5f9d-8fe6-0eaf11538d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05afd7a7-372e-5f9d-8fe6-0eaf11538d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450429} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6caa9e2-7fc4-57e0-84db-0278682927f4" :answer-field/key "The method is absolutely stable." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451168} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75636e46-7f14-5059-a314-ce331ac05304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450430} {:answer/id #uuid "2a562ffb-8f60-5832-8dc2-4814364ea999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75636e46-7f14-5059-a314-ce331ac05304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450430} :answer-field/id #uuid "e64ab8c0-b38a-58c0-8fc6-2259dd4a7a0d" :answer-field/key "The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a2ae136-3885-5a0c-a306-c58f92aeffc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333572" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =5,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-5{y}_{n}+(-5{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement “The stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$” is true.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The stability region is $|\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-5Δx|}{|2+5Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-5Δx| & <|2+5Δx| \\\\ (2-5Δx)^{2} & <(2+5Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} & <4+20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -20Δx & <20Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=1,$ and hence statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=1.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$”; “The stability region is $|\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}|<1.$”; “The method is absolutely stable” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47fdb592-ccb0-5a27-a9f6-4a117a16d8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450432} {:answer/id #uuid "8261d00b-6bb2-5c7a-bf77-c98b2013badc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8261d00b-6bb2-5c7a-bf77-c98b2013badc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450433} :answer-field/id #uuid "3550b9e1-bc13-5259-85b8-16f86b83785a" :answer-field/key "The stability function is $R(2Δx)=\\frac{1-Δx}{1+Δx}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451170} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc9aa8c1-b527-51db-a0b8-1e1fecb6dbe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450434} {:answer/id #uuid "d39e8986-273e-5d17-9983-5b093e1d0b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc9aa8c1-b527-51db-a0b8-1e1fecb6dbe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450434} :answer-field/id #uuid "f86ab810-c96b-5a01-9429-4a08b3802158" :answer-field/key "The stability region is $|2{(Δx-1)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451171} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48ebf7bc-6773-52cb-9e1d-bb4019366fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450436} {:answer/id #uuid "2b13e2fc-22f8-5de7-ae6d-12c53fa497d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b13e2fc-22f8-5de7-ae6d-12c53fa497d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450437} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae1ccfb2-aaf7-5ea1-9fb1-c0bb80b56223" :answer-field/key "The method is absolutely stable." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451172} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cecc2d-c1c2-509f-ab8a-0fb2191af0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450438} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfd37c3-0e32-5e53-8542-a90d26540e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07cecc2d-c1c2-509f-ab8a-0fb2191af0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450438} :answer-field/id #uuid "9213d1b0-0b33-5b3d-90f3-0e0c3cbcd9c4" :answer-field/key "The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=2.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66509616-e823-5ab6-81d6-06eb6bc735de" :question/math-academy-id "q-333574" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =2,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-2{y}_{n}+(-2{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-Δx}{1+Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(2Δx)=\\frac{1-Δx}{1+Δx}.$ Hence, statement “The stability function is $R(2Δx)=\\frac{1-Δx}{1+Δx}.$” is true.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-Δx}{1+Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The stability region is $|2{(Δx-1)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$” is false.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|1-Δx|}{|1+Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |1-Δx| & <|1+Δx| \\\\ (1-Δx)^{2} & <(1+Δx)^{2} \\\\ 1-2Δx+(Δx)^{2} & <1+2Δx+(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -2Δx & <2Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=2,$ and hence statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=2.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability function is $R(2Δx)=\\frac{1-Δx}{1+Δx}.$”; “The method is absolutely stable” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d6581fb-1cd9-5f47-8ff9-5979ac885085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450440} {:answer/id #uuid "523d5a9b-bdc7-5943-9f2e-bd9519abc022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d6581fb-1cd9-5f47-8ff9-5979ac885085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450440} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b68c3fb-619e-551d-af87-e5d8bc615d9c" :answer-field/key "The stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1+7Δx/2}{1-7Δx/2}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "883b44a2-bf01-5f77-a3ef-4667d3673879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450442} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d407e9-bdde-51d4-838c-250cb8be5b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7d407e9-bdde-51d4-838c-250cb8be5b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450443} :answer-field/id #uuid "40e6b0e4-794c-511c-a8b6-8176c4d57f20" :answer-field/key "The stability region is $|\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b751b7e-6ae0-51fb-a6ba-d823ccb81759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450444} {:answer/id #uuid "f0455e55-18d0-5a46-be37-2c94e8c15bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0455e55-18d0-5a46-be37-2c94e8c15bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450445} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bd6593-a175-58b8-8df8-17cd965f7378" :answer-field/key "The method is absolutely stable." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451176} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d9c5801-9019-57ca-93c4-8d5e18dac042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450446} {:answer/id #uuid "03fa487c-d0a5-5d09-b69c-f51e9fd595b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d9c5801-9019-57ca-93c4-8d5e18dac042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450446} :answer-field/id #uuid "70e87aa0-6c37-57e0-9829-c738cc1d85a5" :answer-field/key "The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.5.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55adb6e6-36a2-5272-9189-7161d38a92cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-333578" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =7,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-7{y}_{n}+(-7{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{7}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement “The stability function is $R(7Δx)=\\frac{1+7Δx/2}{1-7Δx/2}.$” is false.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(7Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-7Δx/2}{1+7Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The stability region is $|\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-7Δx|}{|2+7Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-7Δx| & <|2+7Δx| \\\\ (2-7Δx)^{2} & <(2+7Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} & <4+28Δx+49(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -28Δx & <28Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement “The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.5.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability region is $|\\frac{2-7Δx}{2+7Δx}|<1.$”; “The method is absolutely stable” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cacad9b6-d96e-5ed3-8941-1b425e4561b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466097} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca4bf25-4a12-5fe7-b403-40cd2d1ae306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cacad9b6-d96e-5ed3-8941-1b425e4561b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466097} :answer-field/id #uuid "52118cf3-1913-5b6f-a6a4-3a44d5057427" :answer-field/key "$τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466892} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16526fc2-0575-5da4-9643-0dde15f08f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466099} {:answer/id #uuid "1b41930f-abcc-5604-be55-86986ebf53ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16526fc2-0575-5da4-9643-0dde15f08f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466099} :answer-field/id #uuid "516c2af8-6bdf-5d44-a25e-66e659569bb0" :answer-field/key "$e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466893} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1fdffc-3d4c-5f3c-b3fd-1c4e03051b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466101} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d8859c-a702-5bff-9977-f3bbc69941ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8d8859c-a702-5bff-9977-f3bbc69941ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466102} :answer-field/id #uuid "a659403e-5966-58dd-8b52-749c8e0a467c" :answer-field/key "$τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81f632f0-dedf-5479-9362-313bdb74c812" :question/math-academy-id "q-333693" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}$ corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $-3(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2}$ corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $(Δx)^{4}=(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}$ corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "325ae0b8-b12f-51fb-bf37-0ad049b140b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450448} {:answer/id #uuid "3f07c521-0c42-5feb-a2f9-a34c8e920b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f07c521-0c42-5feb-a2f9-a34c8e920b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450449} :answer-field/id #uuid "e341d65f-3bb5-5181-bc1e-a5b09f7fbcec" :answer-field/key "The stability function is $R(4Δx)=1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7130268a-ae24-5efa-a2ae-0809fb7aa80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450450} {:answer/id #uuid "18d1796c-d34c-50d6-a020-999c82bd213d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7130268a-ae24-5efa-a2ae-0809fb7aa80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450450} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fbf22b0-0965-5e9a-b603-6445eb41911b" :answer-field/key "The stability region is $|8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451179} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "468f66ec-a0fc-5dc6-b336-b9e20bcaee6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450452} {:answer/id #uuid "0c4e1c5b-7772-5cd1-a2eb-1eaf0ff1d57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "468f66ec-a0fc-5dc6-b336-b9e20bcaee6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450452} :answer-field/id #uuid "a422fa74-2d88-57d0-ad36-8b90134fee2b" :answer-field/key "The method is absolutely stable." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "334d336b-10d6-59d7-aef6-de8a06343ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450454} {:answer/id #uuid "ed16a682-5357-584a-afb8-68366e4bb05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "334d336b-10d6-59d7-aef6-de8a06343ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450454} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb9ec87-bbc0-52e8-8de9-b614a3a68681" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d872692-c2e4-5ebe-a433-d44857eb60bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-333930" :question/problem "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{y}_{n}-4({y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-8{y}_{n}+16{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+8{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(4Δx)=1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement “The stability function is $R(4Δx)=1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}.$” is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(4Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The stability region is $|8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}|<1.$” is false.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}<Δx-\\frac{1}{4} & <\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0<Δx & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is false.\n\n- Finally, since it does not satisfy this inequality, the modified Euler method is unstable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5.$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability function is $R(4Δx)=1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1bff615-1f3e-50ca-b6e8-55a51ea4366e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Stability of Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "978033bf-6d3c-5fc0-9a25-a661031ac38c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450456} {:answer/id #uuid "727e683c-c551-5aeb-b0fb-8f2c3d784e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "727e683c-c551-5aeb-b0fb-8f2c3d784e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450457} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4c3637c-d804-5401-9392-eaf16c8e4c12" :answer-field/key "The stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451182} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bd21065-4ea9-5ce2-8017-159c0a70bc51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450458} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb2ddb5-a367-56d2-a946-7fc336dfab3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cb2ddb5-a367-56d2-a946-7fc336dfab3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450459} :answer-field/id #uuid "f67f1f28-b93a-58e2-b625-3f11d3fe6912" :answer-field/key "The stability region is $|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451183} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6334c2bc-4740-5e7c-81ec-ebf66178be04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450460} {:answer/id #uuid "48d1670c-d889-56be-9a1e-8f5ccf1dec4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6334c2bc-4740-5e7c-81ec-ebf66178be04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450460} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff35c0ce-dadd-50b5-aae0-31384fdaefa4" :answer-field/key "The method is absolutely stable." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451184} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2e93bc-bab4-592a-a59a-128c7b393d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450462} {:answer/id #uuid "77b9ea2d-e848-5bec-b67a-2f543f11e0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77b9ea2d-e848-5bec-b67a-2f543f11e0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450463} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cab212d-a598-58be-a114-76dafe7042a6" :answer-field/key "The method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "632bafc0-a6e4-5e65-b220-22e1ed511764" :question/math-academy-id "q-333968" :question/problem "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =2,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let’s check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation  \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-2{y}_{n}-2({y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{y}_{n}+4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+2{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement “The stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$” is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement “The stability region is $|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$” is true.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}<Δx-\\frac{1}{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0<Δx & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement “The method is absolutely stable” is false.\n\n- Finally, since it satisfies this inequality, the modified Euler method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and hence statement “The method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$” is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is “The stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$”; “The stability region is $|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$”; “The method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Clairaut Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "393bdc2e-0c53-55d1-b42d-f21779c96991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466103} {:answer/id #uuid "cc37c253-ca9d-582a-a042-d4ba0cd193cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "393bdc2e-0c53-55d1-b42d-f21779c96991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466103} :answer-field/id #uuid "63a0ee9f-d140-5766-a139-2ccf0ede2b4a" :answer-field/key "$y={x}^{3}{y}^{′}+x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30fa331e-24c9-5e82-a126-f6b2be1efb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466105} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8a59f0-ab40-5ca8-85e2-b700ce7b070f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30fa331e-24c9-5e82-a126-f6b2be1efb87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466105} :answer-field/id #uuid "42bd73d3-67cb-5237-b6b1-d25f0f7228f2" :answer-field/key "$x{y}^{4}-xy{y}^{′}-{y}^{2}=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f2a89d1-04d6-5233-9814-a549170ce1cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466107} {:answer/id #uuid "8315e054-5367-5c7b-9e40-75f94b3e5e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8315e054-5367-5c7b-9e40-75f94b3e5e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466108} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed7611aa-0014-5bd2-aa34-2729fec9a6ed" :answer-field/key "$y=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "077e1927-c6a2-59ff-bf00-a9d36509bb47" :question/math-academy-id "q-334000" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation $y={x}^{3}{y}^{′}+x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$ is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation $x{y}^{4}-xy{y}^{′}-{y}^{2}=0$ is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx{y}^{3}-x{y}^{′}-y=0.\n$$\n\nAgain, this equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation $y=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}$ is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b10c1f0-1c23-5e6a-a638-00d4206c3941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466109} {:answer/id #uuid "d28a6002-55cd-5f7f-bbe3-33e7f86078a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d28a6002-55cd-5f7f-bbe3-33e7f86078a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466110} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b58cc72-a7dc-5a38-80f8-5fdd3ef64402" :answer-field/key "$Δx=\\frac{0.25}{\\lambda }$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466898} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f3abbc4-dcb2-5435-b51f-36e87dfc500c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466111} {:answer/id #uuid "931ce38c-be5f-547d-9880-9d2b3317aecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f3abbc4-dcb2-5435-b51f-36e87dfc500c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466111} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2297588-9a94-580d-b537-017ece5f690d" :answer-field/key "$Δx=\\frac{3.5}{\\lambda }$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466899} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e908df6-2015-5d31-8b11-a92c49fe763d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466113} {:answer/id #uuid "826c6c52-f84b-564d-a8da-c33eae270dd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e908df6-2015-5d31-8b11-a92c49fe763d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466113} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f7df6f-2935-5529-af4c-9c1d183d5d81" :answer-field/key "$Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda }$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466900} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "680a020b-b7cf-5cda-ac74-7abef601edd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466115} {:answer/id #uuid "4605055f-d4f1-5f54-b932-4a05194abc44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4605055f-d4f1-5f54-b932-4a05194abc44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466116} :answer-field/id #uuid "4312f87c-513a-51c7-bf55-5d257c752a3d" :answer-field/key "$Δx=\\frac{1.5}{\\lambda }$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3f7955cf-0d1a-5a59-88f5-cf30308c87f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334008" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **stable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.25}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.25.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<0.25 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{3.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=3.5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.5.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.5 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $Δx=\\frac{0.25}{\\lambda }$; $Δx=\\frac{1.5}{\\lambda }$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8263a0c-e1a5-56ef-bc86-5821358b0144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466117} {:answer/id #uuid "05204e01-bf0c-5c2d-9c7c-729f4cd63b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05204e01-bf0c-5c2d-9c7c-729f4cd63b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466118} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdbe8d40-3901-5718-9b8a-901ff7497976" :answer-field/key "$Δx=\\frac{1.4}{\\lambda }$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcb6ab65-a5bc-5d0e-b7ca-1c483ceb82a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466119} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ae84e4-2e66-5ef3-aa5d-24b7302632b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2ae84e4-2e66-5ef3-aa5d-24b7302632b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466120} :answer-field/id #uuid "21ab69dc-d9cd-500c-a280-a04c21ffe02c" :answer-field/key "$Δx=\\frac{1.25}{\\lambda }$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466903} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5e69f56-b610-52ae-851b-4d158ab52afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466121} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f2b199-6721-55fa-9b56-f45abc7d679f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5e69f56-b610-52ae-851b-4d158ab52afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466121} :answer-field/id #uuid "1540c7cc-2f96-585b-bb1b-eb406b71178c" :answer-field/key "$Δx=\\frac{2.5}{\\lambda }$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466904} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c8a5adb-4df8-57eb-b6d0-d7ff26a49607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466123} {:answer/id #uuid "d312f937-6578-5108-a7b2-d3fd1aef1c70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d312f937-6578-5108-a7b2-d3fd1aef1c70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466124} :answer-field/id #uuid "59264af1-9ded-5de5-9faa-802b21ed9e90" :answer-field/key "$Δx=\\frac{0.75}{\\lambda }$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f21f7e32-ddad-5b51-8839-e9c4ba026875" :question/math-academy-id "q-334009" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **stable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$  is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.4}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.4.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.4 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.25}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.25.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.25 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.75}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.75.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<0.75 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $Δx=\\frac{1.4}{\\lambda }$; $Δx=\\frac{1.25}{\\lambda }$; $Δx=\\frac{0.75}{\\lambda }$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4823cf5-2656-5616-b4af-193f70c3f2bc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Euler's Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4e6a2e4-a130-5f3a-b5af-59edc314f0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466125} {:answer/id #uuid "999a1a3e-f07f-51c1-89c7-2c2e5ce92eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "999a1a3e-f07f-51c1-89c7-2c2e5ce92eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466126} :answer-field/id #uuid "29ab3db8-e08d-5ad8-a06f-cc382521ee79" :answer-field/key "$Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda }$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466906} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b7b5bcd-cddb-55f0-b269-79a8ab77d160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466127} {:answer/id #uuid "fdaf88bc-383d-5594-a096-e0a34b4de52c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b7b5bcd-cddb-55f0-b269-79a8ab77d160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466127} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb7bad4e-048c-587a-a7df-e955747cbe46" :answer-field/key "$Δx=\\frac{0.4}{\\lambda }$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466907} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "119754ec-a866-5ea0-9294-1804c408bd5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466129} {:answer/id #uuid "97a0407f-8581-544f-91ac-3d5c1b386e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97a0407f-8581-544f-91ac-3d5c1b386e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466130} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4ce22c-631f-5997-9059-e3bcdc6038d9" :answer-field/key "$Δx=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466908} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2866b660-be68-5c27-a884-63d993e90ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466131} {:answer/id #uuid "a67b55b5-3a28-54fb-995f-718d03c297f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a67b55b5-3a28-54fb-995f-718d03c297f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466132} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbb60801-a619-5246-8b60-f108f387db98" :answer-field/key "$Δx=\\frac{5}{\\lambda }$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c9db1fa-3df6-5592-9112-986f10641d50" :question/math-academy-id "q-334014" :question/problem "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.4}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.4.$ Since this value falls within the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <\\lambda Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <0.4<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method must be stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda }$; $Δx=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }$; $Δx=\\frac{5}{\\lambda }$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Clairaut Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3913a3d4-eb18-566a-bba0-5eb9f606eb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466133} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f3ace4-2efc-5676-839c-634bd66c51cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7f3ace4-2efc-5676-839c-634bd66c51cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466134} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0a53404-f7ab-5f76-bd75-b9c7130c6d46" :answer-field/key "$y+2=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466910} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbd442e4-0902-58ba-892a-8e9478d0359a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466135} {:answer/id #uuid "48d0b697-7b2a-5855-af0d-3e6be989700a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbd442e4-0902-58ba-892a-8e9478d0359a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466135} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f6af450-06bf-5301-82fb-477a0c487d35" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466911} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2da6789-c3f5-5c54-8ed3-4ce91f1e911c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466137} {:answer/id #uuid "96e2df3a-07b8-55d7-bdc0-cb002c447b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96e2df3a-07b8-55d7-bdc0-cb002c447b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466138} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab705d56-ae93-53bf-ac98-aa171ae28c68" :answer-field/key "$x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf963632-b648-5646-b884-a63de120bfff" :question/math-academy-id "q-334133" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation $y+2=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}$ is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}-2.\n$$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}-y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′})$ is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={y}^{′}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation $x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-y=0$ is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y+2=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}$; $x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-y=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Clairaut Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e51e43e-a38f-5fc6-9cdc-00d12a6f4897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466139} {:answer/id #uuid "753bc8f8-a068-5a26-9f52-a999c1df5623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e51e43e-a38f-5fc6-9cdc-00d12a6f4897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466139} :answer-field/id #uuid "939efeec-cf43-5812-8c95-540f23d76384" :answer-field/key "$y=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466913} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "959cce45-20da-5d9b-859f-030760d033c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466141} {:answer/id #uuid "ce277bf8-f2ef-5eaa-8866-5a6bfb518fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce277bf8-f2ef-5eaa-8866-5a6bfb518fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466142} :answer-field/id #uuid "de1fdd51-3055-51cf-b97f-fc7d62de75f5" :answer-field/key "$y=x{y}^{′}+{2}^{{y}^{′}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466914} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b61326a6-2187-5ddd-bf3b-6821add112b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466143} {:answer/id #uuid "d800af5a-d74e-5f9b-b5dd-f8d439655d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b61326a6-2187-5ddd-bf3b-6821add112b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466143} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9e36e60-5b1b-56a3-940e-e4623ab4264a" :answer-field/key "${y}^{2}=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{2}{x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "feda7358-5560-5f92-8b57-984ba0bdd378" :question/math-academy-id "q-334135" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation $y=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})$ is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation $y=x{y}^{′}+{2}^{{y}^{′}}$ is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={2}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{2}=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{2}{x}$ is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. It cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=x{y}^{′}+{2}^{{y}^{′}}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5efaf8e-59a8-5820-88aa-22407a4ba9f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Clairaut Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fb57c9c-4654-5fa1-984b-81edca14e433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466145} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c3b034-007d-5e55-946b-10883071e8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2c3b034-007d-5e55-946b-10883071e8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466146} :answer-field/id #uuid "e049e78e-2582-5bff-9283-be8b3d101d95" :answer-field/key "$y=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′}-1)^{4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466916} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35850ba6-0b0d-55bb-a38a-06ba0f6103a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466147} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec81e48-fd4f-58f1-a7cf-8820c8459fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35850ba6-0b0d-55bb-a38a-06ba0f6103a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466147} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b853afe-1bca-5b0e-aad0-04e8b499b9c2" :answer-field/key "$y{y}^{′}-x{y}^{5}-4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466917} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ff71fa-d7a6-53cd-b648-130d12673059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466149} {:answer/id #uuid "afd5c5f9-4353-5718-b290-b0db56b6f5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afd5c5f9-4353-5718-b290-b0db56b6f5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466150} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0eb242d-045c-56ef-9277-c71fe2eb71bb" :answer-field/key "$x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2)-y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d6213f1-60d6-5a67-a158-34abc9070a3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334148" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation $y=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′}-1)^{4}$ is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=({y}^{′}-1)^{4}.\n$$\n\n- Equation $y{y}^{′}-x{y}^{5}-4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{2}$ is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-x{y}^{4}-4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y.\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation $x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2)-y=0$ is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2),\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′}-1)^{4}$; $x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2)-y=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4bfddbc-eab6-5c68-8480-bef738a56270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466151} {:answer/id #uuid "64147964-eec7-52c1-9f57-1c149a73f852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4bfddbc-eab6-5c68-8480-bef738a56270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466151} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f74ff9e-c2b0-5458-991c-e454a3497dc8" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466919} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dfa3625-eb85-5c68-87b3-150c09ab9de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466153} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1fbef8-4d8a-5576-b0b9-bdc5b60be018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a1fbef8-4d8a-5576-b0b9-bdc5b60be018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466154} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4c8e58a-6367-584c-8e40-5f4fdf246ca5" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466920} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc80953-1aab-5c6b-9404-d800b2762c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466155} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a54cc8-2891-5cca-bcb5-3ffe47599973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cc80953-1aab-5c6b-9404-d800b2762c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466155} :answer-field/id #uuid "5faeb251-e31b-580b-bd7e-425b6f76dd02" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d23e4c59-133c-5f47-8ffc-1426beb350c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334199" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-3(3{e}^{3x})+2({e}^{3x}) & =0 \\\\ 8 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x}-3(2{e}^{2x})+2({e}^{2x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b82d7e5d-b0de-5b58-bca4-ac8e729c45f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466157} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab340e6-5353-51c7-b5ea-8a8add792b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ab340e6-5353-51c7-b5ea-8a8add792b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466158} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f48045e-f503-5f5e-a726-08ab9c810cd0" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=0.95$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466922} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8e31da1-181d-5e4f-857f-0501ca32b218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466159} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac6bd13-0772-5c18-bfa7-64856f65b7c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ac6bd13-0772-5c18-bfa7-64856f65b7c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466160} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e161b0-fed7-5810-81f1-2a7e01ce9b27" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=1.2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466923} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e4ceb8f-a388-5519-aadc-917885568bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466161} {:answer/id #uuid "571931ff-cc0a-5f33-897f-276afffae034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "571931ff-cc0a-5f33-897f-276afffae034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466162} :answer-field/id #uuid "d90a7448-b656-5bd0-8615-ca73e640bb2b" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=0.4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466924} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10700099-d2cd-55bb-945c-f724eb06eae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466163} {:answer/id #uuid "40b5a862-ca4b-524f-8192-40e94e1367ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40b5a862-ca4b-524f-8192-40e94e1367ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466164} :answer-field/id #uuid "a55bccd8-2f1f-5b4f-b96a-315698bac866" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=2.3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9207ec17-19e6-5c10-b901-32c1b663c7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334255" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.95,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.95)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.95}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.95}| \\\\  & =0.5128…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.2}| \\\\  & =0.4545…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.4}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.7142…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.3)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+2.3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3.3}| \\\\  & =0.3030…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\lambda Δx=0.95$; $\\lambda Δx=1.2$; $\\lambda Δx=0.4$; $\\lambda Δx=2.3$.\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcf9011c-88d2-522f-b3b2-7f5e58324603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466165} {:answer/id #uuid "7c54ae2f-cbec-5b96-bc9f-83b395505baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcf9011c-88d2-522f-b3b2-7f5e58324603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466165} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b76786e-84b5-5680-8436-15f590623f91" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466926} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e52cab96-26d7-57ef-a7bd-1454284967ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466167} {:answer/id #uuid "08e922da-3532-5a0c-84aa-77682d68a282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08e922da-3532-5a0c-84aa-77682d68a282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466168} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1740d26-3686-55da-8705-422b2c2b00b7" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466927} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b152814-ca69-5689-a07f-ea7a68ee13fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466169} {:answer/id #uuid "800df2b3-aa45-578a-a674-f96d56bef770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b152814-ca69-5689-a07f-ea7a68ee13fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466169} :answer-field/id #uuid "243b5076-4595-54da-af25-5a1ce4f33b19" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4674804-88cb-5499-be8a-d4a228b08c3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334258" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){e}^{2x} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+5{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e465ed0-28fa-5b9f-95ed-79a40660d4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466171} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf2c758-ce80-5412-81b1-ca972c5051a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecf2c758-ce80-5412-81b1-ca972c5051a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466172} :answer-field/id #uuid "88051d80-7e0f-5259-9d11-0f02843706d0" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466929} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01021e8a-2f30-5b1d-a446-fda33121cb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466173} {:answer/id #uuid "50966b7e-00d2-5249-aaf0-4c04b384797c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50966b7e-00d2-5249-aaf0-4c04b384797c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466174} :answer-field/id #uuid "b80788d5-ebcc-59ed-92b0-24cc521fff81" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466930} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d21b577-109d-5642-8668-cb6bc9643365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466175} {:answer/id #uuid "440e17f7-9b90-5761-8be1-b41c114d1c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d21b577-109d-5642-8668-cb6bc9643365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466175} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b49c89f-2bd2-546f-b4fc-5889ac48750d" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce7557df-4bdb-5791-b8df-2efda5a01a24" :question/math-academy-id "q-334260" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x}+9{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x-1} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x-1}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}+9{e}^{3x-1} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x} & {e}^{3x-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3x} & 3{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}-3{e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x-1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{6x-1}-3{e}^{6x-1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b216db04-2d87-5f29-b031-4b8f18aebbe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466177} {:answer/id #uuid "084e80ea-fab8-56b6-840f-252ff612f881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "084e80ea-fab8-56b6-840f-252ff612f881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466178} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d31f03f-7baf-56e7-b1ac-efd300ef99ba" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff9dc928-9b30-5b8d-8c18-90d8a6534ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466179} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb19be9-60e0-5255-a502-12dbdfe06395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eb19be9-60e0-5255-a502-12dbdfe06395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466180} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d0b221-8cd2-5d31-84e5-81ec105281e0" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466933} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22e26dd4-c78f-596b-9c14-f45264b02075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466181} {:answer/id #uuid "c433f3b3-d4f1-587f-a97d-e0085ce49482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c433f3b3-d4f1-587f-a97d-e0085ce49482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466182} :answer-field/id #uuid "98ee50d6-62fb-5851-9ee5-5ce5a71ca168" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbddaa01-5158-5d3b-b619-76268088aef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334266" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2-3x\\cdot 2x+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)-3x(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$” is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ 2x & 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$”; “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff45dd62-df17-5749-8a41-f08eebe6dbbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466183} {:answer/id #uuid "7d206c86-5689-5b2c-b54e-7232892b3bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d206c86-5689-5b2c-b54e-7232892b3bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466184} :answer-field/id #uuid "9891fc66-c238-53f0-a9cc-3f49c6b8b39a" :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466935} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db3e1dcf-3b31-518b-8484-c581cf7c83d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466185} {:answer/id #uuid "c434960a-d94c-5537-a03f-4ed019706c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c434960a-d94c-5537-a03f-4ed019706c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466186} :answer-field/id #uuid "29753554-0e6b-5e44-9b7f-3c704824a70e" :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466936} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6eb2349-e66a-55f0-ab55-a4d8940ad484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466187} {:answer/id #uuid "29697802-a7e8-5fcd-be1a-e444edb2e8bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6eb2349-e66a-55f0-ab55-a4d8940ad484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466187} :answer-field/id #uuid "326f1276-eef3-5446-91bb-9631e7289f2c" :answer-field/key "The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a08136e-69ff-52a7-8d77-c9b9c2b3571c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334278" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be2f9f36-4bc3-5db9-ae26-8dce1224defd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466189} {:answer/id #uuid "4446428e-23ef-57db-a187-269649fada6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4446428e-23ef-57db-a187-269649fada6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466190} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b3302e4-590a-585f-9efe-737e5f99f137" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=0.5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466938} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33380268-d411-5972-b35d-062f27e75035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466191} {:answer/id #uuid "de530d86-79f6-5322-aa3c-bac403d15739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de530d86-79f6-5322-aa3c-bac403d15739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466192} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a19ee8f-e95d-5f97-a534-0e2baa39901f" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=1.5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466939} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46435866-8983-5c83-ad95-ce0d48116513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466193} {:answer/id #uuid "da05e30d-1d4d-5bb4-85a6-726b2277403c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da05e30d-1d4d-5bb4-85a6-726b2277403c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466194} :answer-field/id #uuid "a220f624-4b00-58ea-ab26-1b0bf2dee6db" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=2.5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466940} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d51cd9b2-7a3a-5788-9485-a756a208edfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466195} {:answer/id #uuid "098dc3da-4adc-5add-8a8e-89793bb8c8ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d51cd9b2-7a3a-5788-9485-a756a208edfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466195} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aa76ce0-7dba-57ee-b1c0-5c3c616685c6" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=3.2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f92808c-572e-59a4-9f7a-86aaaf0f9d81" :question/math-academy-id "q-334431" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.5)| & =|1-0.5+\\frac{(0.5)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.5)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.5)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{233}{384}| \\\\  & =0.606…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.5)| & =|1-1.5+\\frac{(1.5)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1.5)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1.5)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{35}{128}| \\\\  & =0.273…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.5)| & =|1-2.5+\\frac{(2.5)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.5)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.5)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{83}{128}| \\\\  & =0.648…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3)| & =|1-3.2+\\frac{(3.2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3.2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3.2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3427}{1875}| \\\\  & =1.827…<1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\lambda Δx=0.5$; $\\lambda Δx=1.5$; $\\lambda Δx=2.5$ only.\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d2f7889-0d1a-5ad2-bd2e-5e715186663a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466197} {:answer/id #uuid "25b33beb-61ea-571f-847c-5f193ff9ffd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25b33beb-61ea-571f-847c-5f193ff9ffd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466198} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d4b01ac-73aa-5a10-8c95-3cdc3649cfc7" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=0.2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466942} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90d1a5f5-fb4f-5faa-b629-8744e24ca527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466199} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a09e2e-7ba5-5580-8554-967bfc478aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8a09e2e-7ba5-5580-8554-967bfc478aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466200} :answer-field/id #uuid "23431144-5486-5927-be89-7c33eb23203f" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=1.8$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466943} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1250d6b3-dc58-525b-bdfe-8a125d091d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466201} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f37f8a-3cb1-5ea2-b0bc-7863d12677f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9f37f8a-3cb1-5ea2-b0bc-7863d12677f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466202} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e425465-cce5-5152-b300-75d15342331c" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=2.7$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466944} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "351ac62c-28a4-58b8-be80-7507c98d0c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466203} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9fe460-d425-5bb1-a974-e2e29c303200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "351ac62c-28a4-58b8-be80-7507c98d0c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466203} :answer-field/id #uuid "12418649-bb4e-5acc-aaaa-9e9a08a34a65" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=6.6$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e489da60-4fc4-5e3e-84ad-c60bd1e817ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-334435" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.2)| & =|1-0.2+\\frac{(0.2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{12\\,281}{15\\,000}| \\\\  & =0.818…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.8)| & =|1-1.8+\\frac{(1.8)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1.8)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1.8)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1427}{5000}| \\\\  & =0.2854<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.7)| & =|1-2.7+\\frac{(2.7)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.7)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.7)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{70\\,307}{80\\,000}| \\\\  & =0.878…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=6.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.2)| & =|1-6.6+\\frac{(6.6)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(6.6)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(6.6)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{236\\,627}{5000}| \\\\  & =47.3254>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\lambda Δx=0.2$; $\\lambda Δx=1.8$; $\\lambda Δx=2.7$ only.\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "151add00-204f-5795-aff9-cceddc26b24b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Stability in Other Methods"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f389a51-0214-55d4-ad4f-3b7e0b58564c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466205} {:answer/id #uuid "e53370b8-d14d-5d4f-aaa9-0a1221942cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e53370b8-d14d-5d4f-aaa9-0a1221942cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466206} :answer-field/id #uuid "243db64e-5b37-5d20-adb8-52ae5dc1b2d9" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda Δx=0.1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466946} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d57217d5-9847-5533-bd5a-4f3c97b5792b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466207} {:answer/id #uuid "6b44008f-dd84-5aed-bd61-5fb06c98c8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b44008f-dd84-5aed-bd61-5fb06c98c8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text