{3}+6(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{3}=4(Δx)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement $τ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+6(Δx)^{6}$ corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.8(Δx)^{2}+5(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.8(Δx)^{2}=0.8(Δx)^{1+1}.$\nSo, statement $τ(Δx)=0.8(Δx)^{2}+5(Δx)^{3}$ corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{4}=9(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$ corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21ffaded-0ccb-5f13-b811-33701f6c5711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466261} {:answer/id #uuid "0396eff4-577a-5f94-9927-4f8eed41fc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0396eff4-577a-5f94-9927-4f8eed41fc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466262} :answer-field/id #uuid "f14c996d-e452-507e-9e46-0a859b3d4fa9" :answer-field/key "$τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466974} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dde4f68b-d7ce-5557-91ee-694beaffacba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466263} {:answer/id #uuid "df314c42-1898-5b47-ae5d-cfb5a8bb2afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df314c42-1898-5b47-ae5d-cfb5a8bb2afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466264} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bc8d993-fe81-5ea2-837e-05e1c8f91a4f" :answer-field/key "$e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466975} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aa778e3-a751-5e3c-bbce-c3d6e285508d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466265} {:answer/id #uuid "a2808125-76aa-5fe5-a9e0-5a2d101ac8e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aa778e3-a751-5e3c-bbce-c3d6e285508d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466265} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3599088-0440-5c81-8639-94ce7a9bf76b" :answer-field/key "$τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1edbdd20-a6fa-5411-be9e-ad5ea2edf0c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334756" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6}$ corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{3}=4(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$ corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$\nSo, statement $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3}$ corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6}$; $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a19bd48-ecf7-5542-aef9-b80c339d0e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466267} {:answer/id #uuid "99d0f073-a9aa-50db-a37a-0b5fbd6f58c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99d0f073-a9aa-50db-a37a-0b5fbd6f58c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466268} :answer-field/id #uuid "647b4cf5-2656-5712-a376-9a24401a1932" :answer-field/key "$e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466977} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f10ecb-6a81-5ba9-9856-6aaaec40c480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466269} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bbd6dd-71be-52f7-bf35-59d8ee250f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08f10ecb-6a81-5ba9-9856-6aaaec40c480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466269} :answer-field/id #uuid "acdbfa81-5891-564f-b38e-9aca9b11f529" :answer-field/key "$e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466978} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80021749-582b-5c45-993a-3e289cf5583b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466271} {:answer/id #uuid "30d77a63-d315-5188-91b1-75b4d236a957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30d77a63-d315-5188-91b1-75b4d236a957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466272} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d1638c3-109d-5aeb-8697-28c36ac866f4" :answer-field/key "$τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b0cbcd0-f13f-5586-9c22-f5d860877a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-334757" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6}$ corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{3}=2(Δx)^{3}.$ So, statement $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$ corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{5}=0.4(Δx)^{4+1}.$ So, statement $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7}$ corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6}$; $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9074c277-7083-52b7-9d92-f90b79edda0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466273} {:answer/id #uuid "3d43ca0e-6b9a-5360-bf7b-fc4b343a42e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d43ca0e-6b9a-5360-bf7b-fc4b343a42e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466274} :answer-field/id #uuid "067cd356-6a52-5fff-bf1f-661ef57405d9" :answer-field/key "$e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466980} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "524b4010-feab-5741-a5b6-c80bea1db028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466275} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9c3d6c-3410-51a0-910d-c381144e9445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb9c3d6c-3410-51a0-910d-c381144e9445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466276} :answer-field/id #uuid "20f6961f-1c69-53d9-869f-79a49c25c997" :answer-field/key "$τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466981} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35d49689-9636-518d-846f-27322705257a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466277} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9bd4fd-fe8f-573e-907e-4e03e6095642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35d49689-9636-518d-846f-27322705257a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466277} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0e616a8-f854-577b-97f9-bd9a20287da4" :answer-field/key "$e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a147217d-4ada-5afd-bf77-eb76079d5271" :question/math-academy-id "q-334761" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4}$ corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.5(Δx)^{4}=0.5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7}$ corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5}$ corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4}$; $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb4d0cae-0177-5a5d-8631-f39b4951db48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450464} {:answer/id #uuid "f3aff59c-97a3-5e6f-875e-4e2796a585cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3aff59c-97a3-5e6f-875e-4e2796a585cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450465} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc3cc60-3787-5506-84dd-27e14fcdcd91" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451186} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd2f5deb-038e-590b-b232-6e3475aa20c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450466} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c56064-c15b-5471-a1cb-2509a6b27af2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd2f5deb-038e-590b-b232-6e3475aa20c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450466} :answer-field/id #uuid "f67c096d-3014-5488-8e50-1ea90c5de739" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451187} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f89fa4-4b0f-54d4-9391-4960fd8fea1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450468} {:answer/id #uuid "43ef8fa4-cb17-5725-b52e-611adaac83b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23f89fa4-4b0f-54d4-9391-4960fd8fea1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450468} :answer-field/id #uuid "22117b13-f654-5ed2-83f1-db1f9066dd85" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{4})$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6c60236-078a-5017-8be7-a68ef13f931d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334844" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)={x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$” is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(1+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is finite, and $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0$” is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x}+3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x\\to 0,$ the term $\\frac{1}{x}$ becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude, while $3x\\to 0.$\nSo\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x}+3x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is infinite, and $f\\ne O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({x}^{4})$ as $x\\to 0$” is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{4}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x\\to 0,$ the term $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+3)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is infinite, and $f\\ne O({x}^{4})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfee73e7-0112-5b10-9471-39a9a18f611b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466279} {:answer/id #uuid "f2522a59-62fc-5f5a-b7eb-96387dca5f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2522a59-62fc-5f5a-b7eb-96387dca5f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466280} :answer-field/id #uuid "c38575ec-7d37-5f69-888c-de6fa827bcaa" :answer-field/key "$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b993091-02a1-5ea4-b695-173f1843de57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466281} {:answer/id #uuid "26c00063-63de-57f4-a9e4-1ac6e46eda75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b993091-02a1-5ea4-b695-173f1843de57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466281} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d737a1f-7f59-55e7-985d-ab977e42c4cc" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466984} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9846bdec-c728-5080-91e0-da12b46ab5e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466283} {:answer/id #uuid "54ae4d8c-42e7-5689-850a-2615c9fb1102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9846bdec-c728-5080-91e0-da12b46ab5e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466283} :answer-field/id #uuid "96efe50c-03ce-5f7d-8d01-83d2857f6dfc" :answer-field/key "$f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05b85b1b-94cc-5955-8bdf-0e035f03c881" :question/math-academy-id "q-334851" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For $f(x)=3x^5-2x$, we have $f(x)\\to0$ as $x\\to0$, so $f$ is bounded near zero and is $O(1)$.\n\nFor statement “$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$”, $|f(x)/x^2|=|3x^3-2/x|$ is unbounded near zero, so $f$ is not $O(x^2)$. For statement “$f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0$”, $|f(x)/x^3|=|3x^2-2/x^2|\\to\\infty$, so $f$ is not $O(x^3)$.\n\nTherefore, statement “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$” is true, and statements “$f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$” and “$f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0$” are false. The correct answer is “$f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$” only."}]
